        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let $H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form $\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ $$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$ This is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric and equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is $\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$. Thus its Weyl unitary is $W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$, $$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$ For every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is $$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$ Here the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and two-point function $$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$ which obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared fields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing rule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields. The relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is $$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$ understood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance. For $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback and one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock lifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ and for the bounded Weyl operators. For translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the second-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector $(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed future cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector at $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions. No point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$ the closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported smooth $f,g$, put $$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$ On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "sigma-finite measure spaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to $L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then: 1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$; 2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
