      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel-step-1-1",
          "claim": "For an integer $M\\ge2$, integration by parts in $\\mathbf x$ gives\n$$(1+|\\mathbf k|^2)^M\\widehat\\phi_t(\\mathbf k)=\\widehat{(1-\\Delta_{\\mathbf x})^M\\phi(t,\\cdot)}(\\mathbf k).$$\nEvery boundary term vanishes because $\\phi$ is Schwartz. For any fixed $L>3$, Schwartz seminorms bound\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|(1-\\Delta_{\\mathbf x})^M\\phi(t,\\mathbf x)|\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\le C_M(\\phi)(1+|t|)^{-2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}(1+|\\mathbf x|)^{-L}d^3\\mathbf x,$$\nso $|\\widehat\\phi_t(-\\mathbf k)|\\le C'_M(\\phi)(1+|t|)^{-2}(1+|\\mathbf k|^2)^{-M}$. The $t$ bound is integrable. In radial $\\mathbf k$ coordinates the shell weight is at most a constant times $r\\,dr$ because $\\omega_\\mu(r)\\ge r$; hence its product with $(1+r^2)^{-M}$ is integrable at infinity and at $r=0$, including $\\mu=0$. Thus the defining integral converges absolutely and is bounded by a finite Schwartz seminorm, proving that $H_\\mu$ is tempered.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel-step-1-2",
          "claim": "For each $\\omega>0$, let $g_\\omega(t)=e^{-i\\omega|t|}/(2\\omega)$. It is continuous at $0$, with $g_\\omega'(0+)=-i/2$ and $g_\\omega'(0-)=+i/2$. For a compactly supported smooth test $\\eta(t)$, integrating by parts separately on $(-\\infty,0)$ and $(0,\\infty)$ cancels the boundary terms involving $\\eta'(0)$ and leaves the derivative jump times $\\eta(0)$; since $g_\\omega''=-\\omega^2g_\\omega$ off $0$, this proves\n$$g_\\omega''+\\omega^2g_\\omega=-i\\delta_0$$\nas a one-dimensional distribution.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel-step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F4, F5] and Fubini's theorem [F7], the positive-frequency two-point kernel is\n$$W_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}e^{-i\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t+i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}$$\nas a distribution. This is the slice of $H_\\mu$ for $t>0$. For $t<0$, replacing $\\mathbf k$ by $-\\mathbf k$ identifies its slice with $W_\\mu(-t,-\\mathbf x);$ the same substitution proves $H_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)=H_\\mu(-t,-\\mathbf x)$. For real tests, the vacuum expectation of the field commutator is $W_\\mu(f,g)-W_\\mu(g,f)$; [F6] identifies it with a distribution supported in the causal cone. Thus the two Wightman orderings agree on spacelike-separated supports. For a compactly supported smooth spatial test supported away from $\\mathbf x=0$, both slice pairings are continuous in $t$ by [F9] and the integrable bound in 1.1, so no additional term supported at $t=0$ occurs away from the coincidence point. The partial Fourier formula itself specifies the extension at coincidence.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel-step-3-1",
          "claim": "The spatial Fourier multiplier of $\\Box-\\mu^2$ is $-\\partial_t^2-|\\mathbf k|^2-\\mu^2=-(\\partial_t^2+\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)^2)$. Applying it to the kernel in 2.1 and using 1.2 gives $i\\delta_0(t)$ times $(2\\pi)^{-3}\\int e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}d^3\\mathbf k$. Fourier inversion [F8] identifies the latter as $\\delta_0(\\mathbf x)$. For $\\mu=0$ the excluded apex has measure zero and the shell weight is locally integrable there, as established in 1.1; it contributes no additional distribution. Therefore $(\\Box-\\mu^2)H_\\mu=i\\delta_0$ in four dimensions.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F8",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
