      "finite_checks": [],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-countable-choice",
          "carrier": "items/def-countable-choice.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following statement. > For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by > $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that > $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most countable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "A1 is used in step 1.1: Countable Choice, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, asserts that every sequence of nonempty sets has a choice function. [[def-countable-choice]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "A1 is used in step 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-scalar-mass-shell.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here $\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions whose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in [[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Fix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate chart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the $(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative $(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly below. Coordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the Fourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length units and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a physical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set $$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$ The future mass shell is $$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$ When $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless parameterization. The scalar shell pairing is $$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$ Define the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by $$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$ where the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by $$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$ The Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor. If $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)= \\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes the standard transform in [[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]], then $$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$ Its inverse is $$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$ Thus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited Schwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization. The proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving $\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is $x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group. This is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the chosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental observation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F1 is used in step 1.1: The shell density is $d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu)$, the phase is $p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, and the metric is $(-,+,+,+)$. [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]\nF1 is used in step 1.2: The shell density is $d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu)$, the phase is $p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, and the metric is $(-,+,+,+)$. [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]\nF1 is used in step 2.1: The shell density is $d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu)$, the phase is $p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, and the metric is $(-,+,+,+)$. [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]\nF1 is used in step 3.1: The shell density is $d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu)$, the phase is $p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, and the metric is $(-,+,+,+)$. [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step 1.1; F1 is used in step 1.2; F1 is used in step 2.1; F1 is used in step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
          "carrier": "items/def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix an integer $n\\ge1$. With the complex smooth-function convention of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]] and multi-indices of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], define $$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|,\\qquad \\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)=\\{f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C):p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)<\\infty\\text{ for all }\\alpha,\\beta\\}.$$ The zero multi-index gives $x^0=1$ and $\\partial^0f=f$. These are actual smooth functions, not equivalence classes. The notation $C_c^\\infty$ uses the support convention in [[def-c-c-and-c-c-infinity-on-rn]]. Pointwise differentiation is linear; thus $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f+g)\\le p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)+p_{\\alpha\\beta}(g)$ and $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(cf)=|c|p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)$. In particular this set is a complex vector space and each $p_{\\alpha\\beta}$ is a seminorm. The zero function belongs to it. Since $p_{00}(f)=0$ forces $f(x)=0$ for every $x$, the family separates functions. No choice principle is needed.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F2 is used in step 1.1: Schwartz space consists of smooth functions with every polynomially weighted derivative bounded. [[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-tempered-distribution",
          "carrier": "items/def-tempered-distribution.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix an integer $n\\geq1$. A **tempered distribution** on $\\mathbb R^n$ is a continuous complex-linear functional $$u:\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C,$$ where Schwartz space and its locally convex topology are those of [[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]] and [[def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence]]. The space of all such functionals is denoted $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^n)$. We write $\\langle u,\\varphi\\rangle=u(\\varphi)$. This pairing is bilinear: the test variable is not conjugated. Thus $u(a\\varphi+b\\psi)= a\\langle u,\\varphi\\rangle+b\\langle u,\\psi\\rangle$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb C$. The zero functional is tempered. Continuity is topological continuity, and, because the Schwartz topology is generated by a countable cofinal family of seminorms, it is equivalently continuity on every convergent sequence. No choice principle is used in this definition.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F3 is used in step 1.1: A tempered distribution is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space, paired bilinearly with its test function. [[def-tempered-distribution]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$ for all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly [[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let $\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of [[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$ is the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in [[def-tempered-distribution]]. For every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus $\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$. For each tensor product, use the same norm completion as [[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the second-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's first-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged; on elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings. Let $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with $H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of the factor pairings. For $n\\ge1$, let $P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and $P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the permutation action and the orthogonal projections of [[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define $H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and $H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with $H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric $n$-particle sectors. The bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct sums $\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\ \\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\ \\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product. Their vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero components. For $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is $a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$, bosonic annihilation is $a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where $C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$ and its tensor extension is the bounded map of [[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation by $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$, fermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction $c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and $c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the creation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the finite-particle subspace; their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics theorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F4 is used in step 2.1: The bosonic Fock pairing is second-variable-linear, with the stated vacuum and finite-particle domain. [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step 2.1"
