        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let $H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form $\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ $$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$ This is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric and equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is $\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$. Thus its Weyl unitary is $W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$, $$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$ For every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is $$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$ Here the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and two-point function $$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$ which obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared fields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing rule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields. The relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is $$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$ understood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance. For $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback and one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock lifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ and for the bounded Weyl operators. For translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the second-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector $(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed future cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector at $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions. No point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F5 is used in step 2.1: For real tests, $\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ and\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle=\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle.$$\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution $$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$ with the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$ behavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then $$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$ the closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported smooth $f,g$, put $$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$ On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy $$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$ Consequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F6 is used in step 2.1: The scalar commutator distribution has support in the closed causal cone and the smeared free fields commute for pairwise spacelike-separated supports. [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 is used in step 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure spaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to $L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then: 1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$; 2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$; 3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the section-integral functions are integrable; and 4. the three integrals agree: $$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F7 is used in step 2.1: Absolutely integrable functions on sigma-finite product spaces may be integrated in either order. [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F7 is used in step 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-fourier-inversion-on-schwartz-space",
          "carrier": "items/thm-fourier-inversion-on-schwartz-space.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume countable choice. For every $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$ and every $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$, $$f(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\widehat f(\\xi)e^{2\\pi ix\\cdot\\xi}\\,d\\xi.$$ The integral is absolutely convergent.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F8 is used in step 3.1: The reciprocal-normalized Fourier inversion formula holds for Schwartz functions, with Countable Choice as stated by its supplier. [[thm-fourier-inversion-on-schwartz-space]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F8 is used in step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "carrier": "items/thm-dominated-convergence.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that $f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single nonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then $f\\in L^1(\\mu)$, $$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$ and hence $$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F9 is used in step 2.1: Dominated convergence applies to the integrable shell bounds used for the time-slice continuity below. [[thm-dominated-convergence]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F9 is used in step 2.1"
        }
