      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-action-equations-step-1-1",
          "claim": "For the scalar action, differentiating its density at $\\phi+\\varepsilon h$ gives\n$$\\delta S_\\phi=-\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\,\\partial_b h+\\mu^2\\phi h\\right)d^4x.$$\nFor each coordinate, the product $\\partial_a\\phi\\,h$ has compact support; one-dimensional integration by parts on that coordinate has zero boundary term, and Fubini [F4] permits integrating the remaining coordinates. Hence\n$$\\delta S_\\phi=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}h\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b\\phi-\\mu^2\\phi\\right)d^4x=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}h(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi\\,d^4x.$$",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-action-equations-step-1-2",
          "claim": "Put $R_D=(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi$. From [F1], $\\beta^\\dagger=\\beta$ and $\\beta^2=I_4$. Each $\\sigma_j$ is Hermitian, so $\\alpha_j^\\dagger=\\alpha_j$; the off-diagonal blocks give $\\alpha_j\\beta=-\\beta\\alpha_j$. Hence $\\gamma^0=\\beta$ is Hermitian and $(\\gamma^j)^\\dagger=(\\beta\\alpha_j)^\\dagger=\\alpha_j\\beta=-\\beta\\alpha_j=-\\gamma^j$, so $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta\\gamma^a$ for every $a$. Varying $\\psi$ and $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$ together along a real parameter gives\n$$\\delta S_D=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\bar\\chi R_D+\\bar\\psi(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\chi\\,d^4x.$$\nIntegrating the second term by parts coordinatewise, with zero boundary terms since $\\chi$ is compactly supported, yields\n$$\\delta S_D=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\bar\\chi R_D+\\left(-i\\partial_a\\bar\\psi\\,\\gamma^a-\\mu\\bar\\psi\\right)\\chi\\,d^4x=2\\operatorname{Re}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\chi^\\dagger\\beta R_D\\,d^4x,$$\nbecause $R_D^\\dagger\\beta=-i\\partial_a\\bar\\psi\\,\\gamma^a-\\mu\\bar\\psi$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-action-equations-step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi=0$, step 1.1 makes every variation vanish. Conversely, if the smooth residual $R_\\phi=(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi$ is nonzero at a point, continuity gives a ball on which it has one strict sign; choosing the nonnegative bump from [F3] inside that ball gives $\\int hR_\\phi\\ne0$. Thus scalar stationarity for all compactly supported real $h$ is equivalent to the Klein–Gordon equation.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-action-equations-step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $R_D=0$, step 1.2 gives stationarity. Conversely, if $R_D(x_0)\\ne0$, continuity gives a ball on which $|R_D|>0$. Choose a nonnegative bump $\\rho$ supported there and positive on a smaller ball [F3], and take the admissible variation $\\chi=\\rho\\,\\beta R_D$. Then\n$$\\delta S_D=2\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\rho\\,|R_D|^2\\,d^4x>0,$$\ncontradicting stationarity. Thus $R_D=0$ pointwise. Its adjoint is $-i\\partial_a\\bar\\psi\\,\\gamma^a-\\mu\\bar\\psi=0$, and conversely that adjoint equation implies $R_D=0$ since $\\beta$ is invertible.",
          "step": "3.1",
