          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], the mathematical scalar shell conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and the second-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. In the fixed inertial coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] on the affine Minkowski model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; these coordinates have length units under [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. The parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and momentum coordinate $\\mathbf k$ have inverse-length units, and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same units. The parameter is not identified here with a physical rest mass. Matrix $h_D$ has inverse-length units; the dimensionless spin matrices have no physical unit assignment in this mathematical item. Use the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. On $\\mathbb C^4=\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C^2$, define $$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j.$$ For a spinor column $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, its Dirac adjoint is $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$. The Clifford contraction is $\\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j$; this contraction uses the effective matrix $-\\eta$ in the relation $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$. Let $\\mathcal H_D$ be the space of measurable functions $u:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb C^4$, modulo equality almost everywhere, with $$\\|u\\|^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger u(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}<\\infty,$$ and pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3$. Define the measurable matrix $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\sum_j\\alpha_j k_j+\\beta\\mu$ and its maximal multiplication operator with domain $D(h_D)=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H_D:h_Du\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. For $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix fields $$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=\\frac12\\left(I_4\\pm\\frac{h_D(\\mathbf k)}{\\omega(\\mathbf k)}\\right).$$ When $\\mu=0$ at $\\mathbf k=0$, set $P_+(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,0)$ and $P_-(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(0,I_2)$. These are complementary rank-two orthogonal projections; the value at this Lebesgue-null point does not change their multiplication operators. Write $M_{P_\\pm}$ for their maximal multiplication operators and $\\mathcal H_\\pm=\\operatorname{ran}M_{P_\\pm}$. The companion matrix lemma on this page proves these are bounded orthogonal projections with closed ranges. The conjugate Hilbert space $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$ has vectors $\\bar u$, scalar multiplication $z\\bar u=\\overline{\\bar z u}$, and pairing $\\langle\\bar u,\\bar v\\rangle=\\langle v,u\\rangle$. Use the sine and cosine definitions of [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]. For a length-coordinate increment $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix multiplier $$W_D(t,\\mathbf k)=\\cos(\\omega t)I_4+i\\frac{\\sin(\\omega t)}{\\omega}h_D(\\mathbf k).$$ At the massless apex set $W_D(t,0)=I_4$. Its unitarity, group law and generator identity are proved by the companion matrix lemma on this page. The symbols $P_+$ and $P_-$ designate the positive- and negative-energy matrix formulas for this fixed free Dirac operator. This is purely mathematical data; no physical spin-statistics postulate or empirical assertion is included.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F1 is used in step 1.2: The Pauli matrices $\\sigma_j$ are Hermitian. The fixed Dirac block representation is\n$$\\beta=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,-I_2),\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j,\\qquad \\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta.$$\n[[def-qft-dirac-projections]]\nF1 is used in step 3.1: The Pauli matrices $\\sigma_j$ are Hermitian. The fixed Dirac block representation is\n$$\\beta=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,-I_2),\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j,\\qquad \\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta.$$\n[[def-qft-dirac-projections]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step 1.2; F1 is used in step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "carrier": "items/def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, with $n\\ge1$. A complex-valued function is smooth if its real and imaginary parts are $C^k$ for every finite $k$, in the sense of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]. Coordinate derivatives act on these two parts separately. We write $\\partial^\\alpha$ for the multi-index convention there, and $\\partial^0\\varphi=\\varphi$. The **test-function space** is $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)=C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb C)$: its members are smooth functions whose support, the closure in $\\Omega$ of their nonzero set, is a compact subset of $\\Omega$. Equivalently, the zero extension to $\\mathbb R^n$ is smooth with compact support contained in $\\Omega$: compactness gives a neighborhood of every boundary point disjoint from the support, where that extension vanishes. Functions are actual functions, not equivalence classes. Pointwise addition and scalar multiplication make this a complex vector space; the support of a sum lies in the union of the two compact supports. The zero function has empty support. If $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ there is just the empty function, which is the zero vector. The topology is specified in subsequent definitions. Distribution pairings throughout this page are complex bilinear, with no conjugation on test functions. One may restrict all constructions to real scalars.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F2 is used in step 1.1: A variation belongs to $\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$; real scalar variations are its real-valued members. [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]\nF2 is used in step 1.2: A variation belongs to $\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$; real scalar variations are its real-valued members. [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]\nF2 is used in step 3.1: A variation belongs to $\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$; real scalar variations are its real-valued members. [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step 1.1; F2 is used in step 1.2; F2 is used in step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
          "carrier": "items/lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $0<r<R$ and $n\\ge 1$. Then there exists a smooth function $\\rho:\\mathbb R^n\\to [0,1]$ such that $\\rho=1$ on $\\overline B_r(0)$ and $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)\\subseteq B_R(0)$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F3 is used in step 2.1: Every open ball contains a nonnegative smooth compactly supported bump that is positive on a smaller concentric ball. [[lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls]]\nF3 is used in step 3.1: Every open ball contains a nonnegative smooth compactly supported bump that is positive on a smaller concentric ball. [[lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step 2.1; F3 is used in step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure spaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to $L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then: 1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$; 2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$; 3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the section-integral functions are integrable; and 4. the three integrals agree: $$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F4 is used in step 1.1: The iterated integrals of the compactly supported smooth products below agree by Fubini's theorem. [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]\nF4 is used in step 1.2: The iterated integrals of the compactly supported smooth products below agree by Fubini's theorem. [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step 1.1; F4 is used in step 1.2"
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "beta-batch-43-authoring",
        "notes": "Checked compact support, the -+++ gamma/adjoint convention, both stationarity directions, and that the spinor variations are commuting complex variations rather than Grassmann variations. Its Dirac and shell suppliers remain draft inputs."
      }
