          "source": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a separable complex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic creation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps $c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for their restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set $$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$ Then $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces, $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal bosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are closed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy $$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$ The fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the whole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space $$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$ This is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does not assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables-step-1-1",
          "claim": "The measure $d\\nu_\\mu$ is sigma-finite by [F9], and $\\omega_\\mu$ and $k_j$ are finite measurable multipliers by [F2]. Applying [F5] with one-particle multiplier vector $(1,\\omega_\\mu,-k_1,-k_2,-k_3)$ gives the self-adjoint scalar operators $N_s,H_s,P_{s,j}$. On sector $n$, their multipliers are $n$, $\\sum_{r=1}^n\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k_r)$, and $-\\sum_{r=1}^n k_{r,j}$. Squaring these multipliers and integrating against the completed product measure yields exactly the stated domains.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F9",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables-step-1-2",
          "claim": "The Dirac data in [F6] satisfy the measurable, finite-multiplier hypotheses of [F7], and [F16] supplies the complementary orthogonal projections and closed ranges required by that carrier. The one-particle measure is sigma-finite: each of the two species copies of R^3 is the countable union of bounded balls with finite Lebesgue measure. Let Y_m be the two species copies restricted to the ball of radius m; each Y_m has finite measure. For fixed n, the product sets Y_m^n have finite product measure and cover X^n as m grows. Their countable union over n and m is a sigma-finite cover of the Fock direct-sum measure. The particle and antiparticle sector multipliers are therefore self-adjoint on the maximal domains in [F7], which are the displayed square-integrability conditions for $H_D$ and each $P_{D,j}$. The field boundedness and equal-time CAR are exactly the inspected interfaces in [F8].",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F16",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For real $f$, [F3] identifies the scalar field on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ with $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)$. The Hilbert space $H_\\mu$ is separable by [F13], so the hypothesis of the Segal-domain result [F4] holds. Multiplication by the nonzero scalar $\\sqrt2$ does not change an operator domain; hence the closure is self-adjoint on the domain in [F4] with $h=K_\\mu f$. If $K_\\mu f=0$, both operators are zero on all Fock space.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F13",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables-step-2-2",
          "claim": "For a normalized $\\Psi\\in D(A)$, Cauchy–Schwarz [F12] gives $|\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle|\\le\\|A\\Psi\\|<\\infty$. Since $A$ is self-adjoint, this expectation is real. The vector $A\\Psi-\\langle A\\rangle_\\Psi\\Psi$ belongs to the Hilbert space because both terms do, so its norm and the stated variance are finite without requiring $\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. By [F14], a product $AB$ acts only on vectors $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ for which $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$; it is not defined merely because both $A$ and $B$ separately exist on a common dense core.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F12",
            "F14",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables-step-2-3",
          "claim": "Let $\\rho$ be a nonnegative normalized Schwartz Gaussian on $\\mathbb R^4$, and set $\\rho_{\\varepsilon,x_0}(x)=\\varepsilon^{-4}\\rho((x-x_0)/\\varepsilon)$. For every Schwartz test $\\phi$, change of variables and dominated convergence [F10] give $\\int\\rho_{\\varepsilon,x_0}(x)\\phi(x)d^4x\\to\\phi(x_0)$, so this is a point-mass approximation. For every fixed shell momentum $p$, the same change of variables gives $K_\\mu\\rho_{\\varepsilon,x_0}(p)\\to e^{ip\\circ x_0}$ and $|K_\\mu\\rho_{\\varepsilon,x_0}(p)|\\le1$. The shell measure has infinite total mass: in radial coordinates, for $r\\ge\\max(1,\\mu)$, $\\omega_\\mu(r)\\le\\sqrt2\\,r$, so its radial density is bounded below by a positive constant times $r\\,dr$, whose integral diverges. Fatou's lemma [F11] therefore gives $\\liminf_{\\varepsilon_m\\downarrow0}\\|K_\\mu\\rho_{\\varepsilon_m,x_0}\\|^2\\ge\\int_{H_\\mu^+}1\\,d\\nu_\\mu=\\infty$. By [F1, F3] and the creation formula on the vacuum, $\\|\\Phi_\\mu(\\rho_{\\varepsilon_m,x_0})\\Omega\\|=\\|K_\\mu\\rho_{\\varepsilon_m,x_0}\\|$, so these vacuum vectors have no finite-norm point-field limit.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F11",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables-step-3-1",
          "claim": "The scalar one-particle space contains a unit vector $e$, for example a normalized indicator of an annulus $1<|\\mathbf k|<2$, whose shell measure is finite and positive by [F2]. The symmetric vectors $e^{\\otimes n}$ are unit vectors in mutually orthogonal sectors. Put $C=(\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^{-4})^{-1/2}$ and $\\Psi_n=Cn^{-2}e^{\\otimes n}$. By [F15], $\\sum n^{-4}$ converges and is positive, so $C$ is well-defined and $\\Psi$ is normalized. Also by [F15], $\\sum n^{-2}<\\infty$, so $\\Psi\\in D(N_s)$ and $N_s\\Psi=(Cn^{-1}e^{\\otimes n})_{n\\ge1}$ is a Fock vector. Its image under one more application of $N_s$ would have squared norm $C^2\\sum_{n\\ge1}1=\\infty$, so $N_s\\Psi\\notin D(N_s)$. By the composition-domain rule [F14] with $A=B=N_s$, $\\Psi\\notin D(N_s^2)$. Its mean and variance are finite by 2.2.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F14",
            "F15",
            "step 2.2",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables-step-4-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\rho$ be a nonnegative normalized Schwartz Gaussian on $\\mathbb R^3$, fix a unit spinor $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, and put $u_\\varepsilon(\\mathbf x)=\\varepsilon^{-3}\\rho((\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0)/\\varepsilon)z$. For every spatial Schwartz test, change of variables and [F10] show that its pairing with $u_\\varepsilon$ tends to the test's value at $\\mathbf x_0$ times $z$, while $\\|u_\\varepsilon\\|_2=\\varepsilon^{-3/2}\\|\\rho\\|_2\\to\\infty$. By [F16], choose countable dense sets in the two Dirac energy ranges. Conjugating the negative-range dense set preserves density, and the Cartesian product of the two dense sets is countable and dense in the Hilbert direct sum. Therefore the one-particle space for the CAR Fock representation is separable and [F17] applies. For $A_\\varepsilon=\\Psi_t(u_\\varepsilon)$, [F8] gives $A_\\varepsilon A_\\varepsilon^\\dagger+A_\\varepsilon^\\dagger A_\\varepsilon=\\|u_\\varepsilon\\|_2^2I$. By [F17], the creation and annihilation terms are bounded, hence so is their sum. Cauchy–Schwarz [F12] gives $\\sup_{\\|x\\|=\\|y\\|=1}|\\langle A_\\varepsilon x,y\\rangle|\\le\\|A_\\varepsilon\\|$, while choosing $y=A_\\varepsilon x/\\|A_\\varepsilon x\\|$ for each unit $x$ with $A_\\varepsilon x\\ne0$ gives equality after taking the supremum over $x$. The adjoint identity swaps $x$ and $y$, so the same two-vector supremum equals $\\|A_\\varepsilon^\\dagger\\|$. For bounded operators $X,Y$, each unit $\\xi$ satisfies $\\|XY\\xi\\|\\le\\|X\\|\\,\\|Y\\xi\\|\\le\\|X\\|\\,\\|Y\\|$ and $\\|(X+Y)\\xi\\|\\le\\|X\\xi\\|+\\|Y\\xi\\|\\le\\|X\\|+\\|Y\\|$; taking suprema gives product submultiplicativity and the triangle inequality. Applying these inequalities to the CAR identity yields $\\|u_\\varepsilon\\|_2^2=\\|A_\\varepsilon A_\\varepsilon^\\dagger+A_\\varepsilon^\\dagger A_\\varepsilon\\|\\le2\\|A_\\varepsilon\\|^2$. Thus $\\|A_\\varepsilon\\|\\ge\\|u_\\varepsilon\\|_2/\\sqrt2\\to\\infty$, excluding a bounded equal-time point-field norm limit.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "F10",
            "F12",
            "F16",
            "F17",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
