        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ be a separable complex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ be its bosonic Fock space and finite-particle domain. For $h\\in H$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the Segal field $$\\phi_0(h):=2^{-1/2}\\bigl(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h)\\bigr).$$ For $h\\ne0$, this symmetric operator is essentially self-adjoint; write $\\Phi(h)=\\overline{\\phi_0(h)}$. If $r=\\|h\\|$, $e=h/r$, $K=e^\\perp$, $G=\\mathcal F_s(K)$, choose a countable orthonormal basis $(u_j)$ of $G$ whose vectors are finite-particle, and use the one-mode splitting of [[lem-qft-fock-one-mode-splitting]] followed by the Hermite unitary $|n\\rangle\\mapsto g_n$ into $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C)$ with pairing $\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline f g$ to write a vector as $(f_j)_{j\\in J}\\in\\ell^2(J;L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C))$. Then the exact domain and action are $$D(\\Phi(h))=\\left\\{(f_j):\\sum_{j\\in J}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+r^2x^2)|f_j(x)|^2\\,dx<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad (\\Phi(h)f)_j(x)=rx f_j(x).$$ For $h=0$, $\\phi_0(0)$ closes to the zero operator on all of $\\mathcal F_s(H)$. Define $\\epsilon(z)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}z^{\\otimes n}/\\sqrt{n!}$ for $z\\in H$. There are unitaries $W(h)$ determined on these total exponential vectors by $$W(h)\\epsilon(z)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|h\\|^2}{4} +\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}\\langle h,z\\rangle_2\\right) \\epsilon\\!\\left(z+\\frac{i h}{\\sqrt2}\\right).$$ They obey $$W(h)W(k)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{i}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2\\right)W(h+k),\\qquad \\langle\\Omega,W(h)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|h\\|^2/4},$$ and $h\\mapsto W(h)$ is strongly continuous. For every $h$, the one-parameter group $t\\mapsto W(th)$ has norm derivative at zero exactly on $D(\\Phi(h))$, with derivative $i\\Phi(h)$ (for $h=0$ this is the zero generator). Thus the displayed bounded Weyl operators have precisely the closed Segal fields as their generators; no point-field or measurement claim is included.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F4 is used in step 2.1: The Segal field $\\Phi(h)$ is self-adjoint with the exact multiplication domain $\\sum_j\\int(1+\\|h\\|^2x^2)|f_j(x)|^2dx<\\infty$ in its stated Fock decomposition. [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]",
