        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space and let $\\boldsymbol\\omega=(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_d):X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a finite-valued measurable map, where $d\\ge1$. Put $H=L^2(X,\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ with the second-variable-linear pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and form the bosonic Fock space $\\mathcal F_s(H)$. For $n\\ge1$, identify $H_s^{(n)}$ with the symmetric subspace of $L^2(X^n,\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}};\\mathbb C)$ by iterating [[lem-qft-l2-product-tensor-identification]]. Set $X^0=\\{\\star\\}$, $s_0(\\star)=0$, and for $n\\ge1$ set $$s_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\sum_{r=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\omega(x_r)\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$ For $1\\le j\\le d$, define $H_j$ by multiplication by the $j$th coordinate $s_{n,j}$ on sector $n$, with exact domain $$D(H_j)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n): \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{X^n}|s_{n,j}|^2|\\Psi_n|^2\\,d\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}}<\\infty\\right\\}.$$ Each $H_j$ is self-adjoint, and $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\cap D(H_j)$ is a graph core. The joint projection-valued measure [[def-projection-valued-measure]] on $\\mathbb R^d$ is $$(E(B)\\Psi)_n=\\mathbf1_{s_n^{-1}(B)}\\Psi_n\\quad(n\\ge1),\\qquad (E(B)\\Psi)_0=\\mathbf1_B(0)\\Psi_0.$$ For $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^d$, let $H_\\theta$ be multiplication on sector $n$ by $\\theta\\cdot s_n$, with its maximal square-integrability domain. Then $$U_\\theta(t)\\Psi:=\\bigl(e^{it\\theta\\cdot s_n}\\Psi_n\\bigr)_{n\\ge0}$$ is a strongly continuous unitary group on all of Fock space, and its norm derivative at zero exists exactly for $\\Psi\\in D(H_\\theta)$, where it is $iH_\\theta\\Psi$. The zero-particle sector is the vacuum eigenvector at joint value $0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F5 is used in step 1.1: On bosonic Fock space, a measurable finite real multiplier $\\boldsymbol\\omega$ gives self-adjoint sector multipliers by $s_n=\\sum_{r=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\omega(x_r)$, with exact square-integrability domains and the corresponding joint PVM. Taking $\\boldsymbol\\omega=(1,\\omega_\\mu,-k_1,-k_2,-k_3)$ gives $N_s,H_s,P_{s,j}$. [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]\nF5 is used in step 3.1: On bosonic Fock space, a measurable finite real multiplier $\\boldsymbol\\omega$ gives self-adjoint sector multipliers by $s_n=\\sum_{r=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\omega(x_r)$, with exact square-integrability domains and the corresponding joint PVM. Taking $\\boldsymbol\\omega=(1,\\omega_\\mu,-k_1,-k_2,-k_3)$ gives $N_s,H_s,P_{s,j}$. [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step 1.1; F5 is used in step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-dirac-projections.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], the mathematical scalar shell conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and the second-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. In the fixed inertial coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] on the affine Minkowski model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; these coordinates have length units under [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. The parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and momentum coordinate $\\mathbf k$ have inverse-length units, and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same units. The parameter is not identified here with a physical rest mass. Matrix $h_D$ has inverse-length units; the dimensionless spin matrices have no physical unit assignment in this mathematical item. Use the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. On $\\mathbb C^4=\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C^2$, define $$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j.$$ For a spinor column $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, its Dirac adjoint is $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$. The Clifford contraction is $\\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j$; this contraction uses the effective matrix $-\\eta$ in the relation $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$. Let $\\mathcal H_D$ be the space of measurable functions $u:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb C^4$, modulo equality almost everywhere, with $$\\|u\\|^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger u(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}<\\infty,$$ and pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3$. Define the measurable matrix $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\sum_j\\alpha_j k_j+\\beta\\mu$ and its maximal multiplication operator with domain $D(h_D)=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H_D:h_Du\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. For $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix fields $$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=\\frac12\\left(I_4\\pm\\frac{h_D(\\mathbf k)}{\\omega(\\mathbf k)}\\right).$$ When $\\mu=0$ at $\\mathbf k=0$, set $P_+(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,0)$ and $P_-(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(0,I_2)$. These are complementary rank-two orthogonal projections; the value at this Lebesgue-null point does not change their multiplication operators. Write $M_{P_\\pm}$ for their maximal multiplication operators and $\\mathcal H_\\pm=\\operatorname{ran}M_{P_\\pm}$. The companion matrix lemma on this page proves these are bounded orthogonal projections with closed ranges. The conjugate Hilbert space $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$ has vectors $\\bar u$, scalar multiplication $z\\bar u=\\overline{\\bar z u}$, and pairing $\\langle\\bar u,\\bar v\\rangle=\\langle v,u\\rangle$. Use the sine and cosine definitions of [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]. For a length-coordinate increment $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix multiplier $$W_D(t,\\mathbf k)=\\cos(\\omega t)I_4+i\\frac{\\sin(\\omega t)}{\\omega}h_D(\\mathbf k).$$ At the massless apex set $W_D(t,0)=I_4$. Its unitarity, group law and generator identity are proved by the companion matrix lemma on this page. The symbols $P_+$ and $P_-$ designate the positive- and negative-energy matrix formulas for this fixed free Dirac operator. This is purely mathematical data; no physical spin-statistics postulate or empirical assertion is included.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F6 is used in step 1.2: The Dirac matrices fix $\\omega(\\mathbf k)$, $h_D$, $P_\\pm$, the particle and antiparticle one-particle spaces, and their Hilbert measures. [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 is used in step 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]] and [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]]. Define $\\chi(s)=\\cos s+i\\sin s$ for real $s$, using [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]. Let $X=(\\{+\\}\\times\\mathbb R^3)\\sqcup(\\{-\\}\\times\\mathbb R^3)$ carry counting measure on the species label and $d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3$ on each copy. The fiber $\\mathcal V_x$ is $\\mathbb C^4$ for $x=(+,\\mathbf k)$ and $\\overline{\\mathbb C^4}$ for $x=(-,\\mathbf k)$; $L^2(X;\\mathcal V)$ denotes measurable sections with square-integrable fiber norm, and is the direct sum $L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)\\oplus L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\overline{\\mathbb C^4})$. Identify $\\mathcal K=\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$ with the closed subspace whose $+$ fibers lie in $\\operatorname{ran}P_+(\\mathbf k)$ and whose $-$ fibers lie in $\\overline{\\operatorname{ran}P_-(\\mathbf k)}$; form its antisymmetric Fock space $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$ using [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. For $x=(+,\\mathbf k)$ set $q(x)=(\\omega(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k)$, and for $x=(-,\\mathbf k)$ set $q(x)=(\\omega(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. The one-particle translation multipliers $V(a)u(x)=\\chi(q^0(x)a^0-\\mathbf q(x)\\cdot\\mathbf a)u(x)$ preserve $\\mathcal K$ and lift to a strongly continuous unitary representation on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$. Write $U_j(t)$ for its Fock lift along the $j$th coordinate axis of $\\mathbb R^4$. On the $n$-particle sector, let $s_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\sum_{r=1}^n q(x_r)$ and set $s_0=0$ on the vacuum sector. The joint spectral measure is multiplication by $\\mathbf1_{s_n^{-1}(B)}$ on each sector, restricted to its antisymmetric subspace. Its support is contained in the closed future cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf p):E\\ge|\\mathbf p|\\}$. Define the time generator $H$ and spatial momentum components $P_j$ by $U(te_0)=\\chi(tH)$ and $U(te_j)=\\chi(-tP_j)$, where $e_\\alpha$ are the coordinate vectors in translation space. Then $H$ and each $P_j$ are the maximal real multipliers by $s_{n,0}$ and $s_{n,j}$, respectively, with domains $\\{\\Psi:\\sum_n\\|s_{n,\\alpha}\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$; the minus sign in spatial translation phases is the Minkowski pairing convention in $V(a)$. The vacuum is the unique zero-energy vector; for $\\mu>0$ the nonvacuum spectrum has gap at least $\\mu$, while for $\\mu=0$ it has no positive gap.",
