        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For $F\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4)$, define $$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$ Then $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and $$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$ defines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies $$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$ The positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are $$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$ Their tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below. Its tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$. The spacetime anticommutator kernel is $$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$ where $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
