        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the space $H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu)$ of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] is separable, and $$\\{K_\\mu f:f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)\\}$$ is dense in $H_\\mu$. If $\\mu>0$, the range contains every function in $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$; if $\\mu=0$, it contains every function in $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F13 is used in step 2.1: The scalar shell Hilbert space $H_\\mu$ is separable for every $\\mu\\ge0$. [[lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F13 is used in step 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-nrqm-uncertainty-schrodinger-robertson-anticommutator-bound",
          "carrier": "items/lem-nrqm-uncertainty-schrodinger-robertson-anticommutator-bound.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space with inner product linear in the first argument ([[def-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]]), let $A,B$ be symmetric densely defined operators on $H$ ([[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]], [[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]]), and let $u\\in D(A)\\cap D(B)$ with $\\|u\\|=1$ satisfy in addition $u\\in D(AB)\\cap D(BA)$, that is $Bu\\in D(A)$ and $Au\\in D(B)$. Put $$a:=\\langle Au,u\\rangle,\\qquad b:=\\langle Bu,u\\rangle,\\qquad x:=(A-a)u,\\qquad y:=(B-b)u,\\qquad \\Delta A:=\\|x\\|,\\qquad \\Delta B:=\\|y\\|,$$ and write $\\{A,B\\}u:=ABu+BAu$ and $[A,B]u:=ABu-BAu$ for the pairings at $u$. Then: (a) $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$, $x,y\\in H$, and $$\\operatorname{Re}\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\bigl\\langle\\{(A-a),(B-b)\\}u,u\\bigr\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\langle\\{A,B\\}u,u\\rangle-ab, \\qquad \\langle[A,B]u,u\\rangle=-2i\\operatorname{Im}\\langle x,y\\rangle ;$$ (b) *(Robertson bound)* $\\Delta A\\,\\Delta B\\ge\\tfrac12\\bigl|\\langle[A,B]u,u\\rangle\\bigr|$; (c) *(Schroedinger-Robertson bound)* $$(\\Delta A)^{2}(\\Delta B)^{2}\\ge\\Bigl(\\tfrac12\\bigl\\langle\\{(A-a),(B-b)\\}u,u\\bigr\\rangle\\Bigr)^{2}+\\tfrac14\\bigl|\\langle[A,B]u,u\\rangle\\bigr|^{2},$$ that is, with the expanded anticommutator pairing $(\\Delta A)^{2}(\\Delta B)^{2}\\ge\\bigl(\\tfrac12\\langle\\{A,B\\}u,u\\rangle-ab\\bigr)^{2}+\\tfrac14\\bigl|\\langle[A,B]u,u\\rangle\\bigr|^{2}$; (d) equality holds in (c) if and only if $x$ and $y$ are linearly dependent; in particular the Robertson bound (b) follows from (c) because the first square is nonnegative; (e) if $A$ and $B$ are bounded everywhere-defined self-adjoint operators, then the product-domain hypothesis $u\\in D(AB)\\cap D(BA)$ holds for every unit $u\\in H$ and clauses (a)-(d) apply to every unit $u$. No energy-time inequality and no apparatus statement is made.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F14 is used in step 2.2: For symmetric operators $A,B$, $\\Psi\\in D(AB)$ means $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ and $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$. [[lem-nrqm-uncertainty-schrodinger-robertson-anticommutator-bound]]\nF14 is used in step 3.1: For symmetric operators $A,B$, $\\Psi\\in D(AB)$ means $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ and $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$. [[lem-nrqm-uncertainty-schrodinger-robertson-anticommutator-bound]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier; statement and proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F14 is used in step 2.2; F14 is used in step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-p-series-real-exponents",
          "carrier": "items/thm-p-series-real-exponents.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For every real $p$, $$\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\frac1{k^p}\\text{ converges}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad p>1.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F15 is used in step 3.1: The series $\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^{-p}$ converges for $p>1$ and diverges for $p\\le0$. [[thm-p-series-real-exponents]]",
