        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and use the definitions in [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$ and almost every $\\mathbf k$, the displayed Pauli blocks satisfy $h_D(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger=h_D(\\mathbf k)$, $h_D(\\mathbf k)^2=\\omega(\\mathbf k)^2I_4$, and $P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)$ are complementary orthogonal projections of rank two. The ranges $\\mathcal H_\\pm$ are closed separable Hilbert subspaces of $\\mathcal H_D$, and $h_D$ is self-adjoint on $D(h_D)=\\{u:\\omega u\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. The matrices also satisfy $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ and $\\gamma^{0\\dagger}=\\gamma^0$, $\\gamma^{j\\dagger}=-\\gamma^j$. For a unit spatial vector $\\mathbf n$, put $\\Sigma_{\\mathbf n}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\cdot\\mathbf n,\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\cdot\\mathbf n)$, $S_R(\\theta)=\\cos(\\theta/2)I_4-i\\sin(\\theta/2)\\Sigma_{\\mathbf n}$, and $S_B(\\chi)=\\cosh(\\chi/2)I_4+\\sinh(\\chi/2)\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf n$. With $R_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)$ the rotation $\\mathbf v_\\parallel+\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf v_\\perp+\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v_\\perp$ using [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], with $\\mathbf v_\\parallel=(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n)\\mathbf n$ and $\\mathbf v_\\perp=\\mathbf v-\\mathbf v_\\parallel$, and $B_{\\mathbf n}(\\chi)$ the boost defined in [[lem-qft-lorentz-boost-rotation-factorization]], these matrices satisfy $S_R\\Gamma(p)S_R^{-1}=\\Gamma(R_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)p)$, $S_B\\Gamma(p)S_B^{-1}=\\Gamma(B_{\\mathbf n}(\\chi)p)$, and $S_R\\beta S_R^\\dagger=S_B\\beta S_B^\\dagger=\\beta$. For each real $t$, $W_D(t,\\mathbf k)$ of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]] is unitary, satisfies $W_D(t+s,\\mathbf k)=W_D(t,\\mathbf k)W_D(s,\\mathbf k)$, and obeys $\\partial_tW_D(t,\\mathbf k)=i h_D(\\mathbf k)W_D(t,\\mathbf k)$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F16 is used in step 1.2: The Dirac projection matrix lemma establishes complementary orthogonal projections and closed separable ranges for the two Dirac energy subspaces. [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]]\nF16 is used in step 4.1: The Dirac projection matrix lemma establishes complementary orthogonal projections and closed separable ranges for the two Dirac energy subspaces. [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F16 is used in step 1.2; F16 is used in step 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-fock-ccr-car.md",
          "status": "current draft mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a separable complex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic creation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps $c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for their restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set $$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$ Then $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces, $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal bosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are closed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy $$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$ The fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the whole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space $$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$ This is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does not assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "F17 is used in step 4.1: For a separable one-particle Hilbert space, the fermionic creation and annihilation maps are bounded and satisfy the canonical anticommutation relations. [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof status; only the inspected current statement and proof interface is consumed conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F17 is used in step 4.1"
        }
