          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Fix the scalar shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and all shell, Fourier, and metric conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $\\Box=-\\partial_t^2+\\Delta_{\\mathbf x}$. For $\\phi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define the partial spatial Fourier transform $$\\widehat\\phi_t(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\phi(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x$$ and define $H_\\mu\\in\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$ by $$\\langle H_\\mu,\\phi\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R}dt\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}e^{-i\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)|t|}\\widehat\\phi_t(-\\mathbf k).$$ This is a continuous distributional extension of the free scalar time-ordered two-point function: on $t>0$ its kernel is the Wightman two-point distribution $W_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)$, and on $t<0$ it is $W_\\mu(-t,-\\mathbf x)$. The displayed partial Fourier formula fixes the extension at $t=0$; no product of a step function with an unspecified singular distribution is used. The kernel is symmetric under $(t,\\mathbf x)\\mapsto(-t,-\\mathbf x)$, agrees with the two orderings on spacelike-separated supports, and obeys $$ (\\Box-\\mu^2)H_\\mu=i\\delta_0 $$ in $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$. There is no extra prefactor $i$ in the definition of $H_\\mu$.",
